{"name":"Freckle Face","hex":"#9b6f55","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"freckle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small brownish or reddish pigmentation spot on the surface of the skin.","Any small spot which has a different colour from its background."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover (someone; or something, such as the skin) with freckles (noun sense 1 and noun sense 2).","To become covered with freckles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/freckle"},{"term":"face","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The front part of the head of a human or other animal, featuring the eyes, nose, and mouth, and the surrounding area.","One's facial expression."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To position oneself or itself so as to have one's face closest to (something).","To have its front closest to, or in the direction of (something else)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/face"}],"entries":[{"title":"Freckle","blocks":[{"text":"Freckles are clusters of concentrated melaninized cells which are most easily visible on people with a fair complexion. Freckles do not have an increased number of the melanin-producing cells, or melanocytes, but instead have melanocytes that overproduce melanin granules (melanosomes) changing the coloration of the outer skin cells (keratinocytes). As such, freckles are different from lentigines and moles, which are caused by accumulation of melanocytes in a small area. Freckles can appear on all types of skin tones. Of the six Fitzpatrick skin types, they are most common on skin tones 1 and 2, which usually belong to North Europeans. However, it can also be found on people all over the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Vesnuschki.jpg/330px-Vesnuschki.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Freckle","wikidata":"Q187882"},{"title":"Face","blocks":[{"text":"The face is \"the front part of the head of a human or other animal, featuring the eyes, nose and mouth, and the surrounding area.\" While that basic structure is found in many animals, the evolutionary development of mammals has led to a wide diversity of facial types between those species, identifiable by characteristics of facial features and expressive behaviors."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Human_faces.jpg/330px-Human_faces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Face","wikidata":"Q37017"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Freckled.jpg/500px-Freckled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Freckled.jpg/500px-Freckled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Women with freckles","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFreckled.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/FrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg/500px-FrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/FrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg/1280px-FrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/FrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg/500px-FrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Freckles on a female face after summertime","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrecklesAfterSummertime.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Sobo_1909_260.png/500px-Sobo_1909_260.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons